This is interesting.  So, how to get the amulet off the kobold
without it being used?

Will the monster pick up and try on other amulets, as they do
with weapons?  ("The dwarf wields a dagger!" <move> "The dwarf
wields a crossbow!")  In that case, it could be killed and
retrieved while the monster is merely holding it.
What happens if a species of monster is genocided right in front
of you...does their equipment remain in a pile on the floor?
Second possible way.

But there's still no way to tell if a monster *has* an amulet
in most circumstances, or has any other type of equipment.
I really believe that a new ability to scan the outward
appearance of a monster is in order, with the increasing
complexity of the game.  I suggest the following:

1.)  A limited ability for all character classes in all conditions
to see the most basic of a monster's equipment, including wielded
weapon (or weapons for multi-handed creatures), and outside armor
(but not mail-underneath-cloak, for example).  Plusses and blessed
state would be unknown.  This information would be accessed by
the '/' or ';' commands.  In dark areas the information could be
incomplete or even unreliable, and perhaps unreliable also
while confused or hallucinating.  Glimpses of rings, but not
ring type, could be possible, if the ability for monsters to wear
rings is ever added.  Assuming amulets are worn over the armor,
amulet type is made known; if under the armor, then nothing
is known.  Wands are difficult...where are wands when they're
not being used?  Information becomes less complete based on the
distance to the monster.

2.)  An extended ability, granted by a new type of directional
wand, to see a monster's entire inventory.  This should be
separated from the wand of probing to avoid making it too
powerful, though perhaps that isn't necessary.  It could also
be a spell, and perhaps even an intrinsic.  Some character
classes could gain information somewhere between levels 1)
and 2).  Priests could have an ability to detect blessed
and cursed objects on a monster.  Thieves could ascertain
some of the inventory of sleeping or confused monsters with
#loot, with a chance of waking them up, and perhaps even of
stealing something.


If there's going to be monsters carrying deadly wands (sleep,
death) and, it appears, powerful magic items (amulet of
life-saving, magic lamp), allow the player another way to
gain an ability, to deal with this new factor and make decisions
strategically with the ability.  In my opinion, there's
too much hinging on the ability to get reflection right now.
Putting on one SoR or AoR gives the player the power to ignore
many of the most deadly attacks in the game.  Instead, make
reflection an unsure thing, forcing the player to break up
the rather simple emphasis on gaining reflection into new
and conflicting goals of gaining the "inventory" ability
(so that he may avoid or deal otherwise with wand-carrying
monsters), using the reflection shield or amulet sparingly
(so that it not be overtaxed, and it wouldn't work all the
time anyway), and more.
If you can see that a monster has a lamp, you can decide
if you want to kill it right away before it has a chance
to use it and possibly waste one of your wishes.  If
you can see it has an amulet on, you can decide not to kill
it and gain the amulet some other way, in order not
to possible waste a life-save.  If you can see that
a monster coming at you has a big old nasty +4 long sword,
and you *just* got out of a fight that nearly killed you
and you're trying to recover hit points, you can decide
not to fight that particular one, right now.  The
strategic possibilities are enormous.

